        "findings": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe structure is clean and an unusually faithful match to this codebase's established conventions. Verified against the existing units:\n\n- **`OwnerPrimaryIdentifier` is in the right unit and idiomatic.** It replicates the exact shape of `MembershipNumber`, `OwnerIdentity`, and `CustomerCode` in the `model` package: `final` class, private constructor, static `of(Owner)`, and the same \"single source of truth / model-level derivation of an `Owner`\" javadoc framing. This is precisely where this codebase puts \"the one derived value every consumer reads.\" It also localizes the spec's forward-looking migration (\"when customerCode is unified into memberId, the event carries the memberId instead\") to a single method \u2014 exactly the intent of the pattern, and not redundant with `CustomerCode` (that class is about the code's *format/derivation*; this one is about *which field is primary*).\n\n- **`OwnerCreatedEvent` is well-placed and cohesive.** A pipeline-local, immutable event DTO sitting beside `AuditOwnerCreated` in `rest/function/owner` is the natural home (DTOs/events at the edges, per the house rule). The convenience constructor defaulting the `OWNER_CREATED` discriminator and the documented canonical field order are tidy, not over-built.\n\n- **`AuditOwnerCreated` stays coherent rather than becoming a catch-all.** The added responsibilities (seq counter, event construction, JSON emission) are all tightly scoped to \"audit the owner-created event,\" and emitting on the same `AUDIT` logger keeps it one concern. A shared static `ObjectMapper` and a process-wide `AtomicLong SEQ` are conventional, localized choices for a stateless pipeline function.\n\nMinor, non-blocking observations (no change needed):\n- `MembershipLevel.of(owner)` is evaluated twice in `service()` \u2014 once for the line, once for the event \u2014 but hoisting it to a local would be a micro-optimization, not a structural fix.\n- The event field is named `customerCode` while being fed `OwnerPrimaryIdentifier.of(...)`, so after the memberId migration the key/value names will diverge. This is spec-mandated (the JSON key is fixed as `customerCode` while its value becomes the memberId) and is explicitly documented in both javadocs, so it's a deliberate, recorded tension rather than a defect.\n\nA maintainer of this repo would find this change natural and consistent."
